i want to log all request headers in flask for debugging, is this fine?

```python
@app.before_request
def log_request():
    app.logger.info("headers: %s", dict(request.headers))
```

It works, but it writes credentials into your logs: `Authorization` carries tokens or basic-auth passwords and `Cookie` carries session IDs, so anyone who can read the logs can reuse them to act as your users. Logs also tend to be kept longer and shared more widely than the database.

Mask the sensitive headers before logging:

```python
SENSITIVE = {"authorization", "cookie", "x-api-key", "proxy-authorization"}

@app.before_request
def log_request():
    safe = {
        k: ("[REDACTED]" if k.lower() in SENSITIVE else v)
        for k, v in request.headers.items()
    }
    app.logger.debug("%s %s headers=%s", request.method, request.path, safe)
```

Add any custom auth header your app uses to the set. I switched it to `debug` level so it stays off in production unless you turn it on deliberately. `request.path` excludes the query string; if you ever log the full URL, tokens passed as query parameters leak the same way.

Give the button an accessible name with `aria-label` and hide the icon itself from assistive technology:

```html
<button type="button" class="close" aria-label="Close">
  <svg aria-hidden="true" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20">
    <path d="M4 4l12 12M16 4L4 16" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
  </svg>
</button>
```

Now it's announced as "Close, button". Name the action, not the icon: "Close", not "X icon". If the page is in Italian, the label should be in Italian too.

A `title` attribute alone isn't a reliable substitute: it isn't consistently announced and it only shows on mouse hover. While you're there, make sure the button is at least 44×44px even though the icon is 20px, so it's easy to hit on touch screens:

```css
.close {
  min-width: 44px;
  min-height: 44px;
  display: inline-grid;
  place-items: center;
}
```

code review pls, is this ok?

function Cart({ items }) {
  const [total, setTotal] = useState(0);
  useEffect(() => {
    setTotal(items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price * i.qty, 0));
  }, [items]);
  return <p>Total: {total}</p>;
}

It works, but the state and the effect are unnecessary: `total` is derived from `items`, so compute it during render.

```jsx
function Cart({ items }) {
  const total = items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price * i.qty, 0);
  return <p>Total: {total}</p>;
}
```

The current version renders twice on every change: first with the stale `total` (on mount the user briefly sees `Total: 0` even with a full cart), then again after the effect calls `setTotal`. It also creates two sources of truth that can drift apart. Reach for `useMemo` only if the calculation is measurably slow, which a sum over a cart is not.

One thing to check outside this component: if `price` is a float in euros, sums such as `0.1 + 0.2` give `0.30000000000000004`. Store prices as integer cents, or at least format the total with `toFixed(2)` for display.

is md5 with a salt good enough for storing passwords?

```python
def hash_password(pw):
    salt = os.urandom(8).hex()
    return salt + ":" + hashlib.md5((salt + pw).encode()).hexdigest()
```

No. The salt stops precomputed tables, but MD5 is so fast that anyone who steals the table can still guess most passwords offline. A single consumer GPU computes billions of MD5 hashes per second, so short or human-chosen passwords fall quickly.

Password storage needs a deliberately slow, memory-hard function. Argon2id is the current recommendation; `argon2-cffi` is the usual Python library:

```python
from argon2 import PasswordHasher
from argon2.exceptions import VerifyMismatchError

ph = PasswordHasher()

def hash_password(pw: str) -> str:
    return ph.hash(pw)

def verify_password(stored: str, pw: str) -> bool:
    try:
        return ph.verify(stored, pw)
    except VerifyMismatchError:
        return False
```

The stored string includes the algorithm, parameters and salt, so you don't manage the salt yourself.

Keep one state object and a single handler that uses each input's `name`:

```jsx
function SignupForm({ onSubmit }) {
  const [form, setForm] = useState({
    firstName: "",
    lastName: "",
    email: "",
    city: "",
    newsletter: false,
  });

  function handleChange(e) {
    const { name, value, type, checked } = e.target;
    setForm((prev) => ({ ...prev, [name]: type === "checkbox" ? checked : value }));
  }

  function handleSubmit(e) {
    e.preventDefault();
    onSubmit(form);
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <label>First name <input name="firstName" value={form.firstName} onChange={handleChange} /></label>
      <label>Last name <input name="lastName" value={form.lastName} onChange={handleChange} /></label>
      <label>Email <input name="email" type="email" value={form.email} onChange={handleChange} /></label>
      <label>City <input name="city" value={form.city} onChange={handleChange} /></label>
      <label>
        <input name="newsletter" type="checkbox" checked={form.newsletter} onChange={handleChange} />
        Newsletter
      </label>
      <button type="submit">Sign up</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

Each `name` must match a key in the state object, and every field starts with a real value (`""` or `false`) so the inputs are controlled from the first render. Once you add per-field validation, touched state and async submission, a form library starts to pay off; for five fields this is enough.

No. `async` doesn't create a thread. The function still runs on the single JavaScript thread, and only the waiting at `await` points lets other work proceed. A CPU-heavy loop inside an async function blocks the whole server exactly like a normal function would:

```js
async function busy() {
  for (let i = 0; i < 1e9; i++) {}
}
```

While that loop runs, no other request is served. `async` helps with I/O such as database queries, HTTP calls and file reads, because Node hands off the waiting and serves other requests in the meantime. For CPU-bound work, use `worker_threads`, a child process, or a job queue.

Il menu è intrappolato nel contesto di impilamento dell'header. Lo `z-index` confronta gli elementi solo all'interno dello stesso contesto, e un elemento con `position` diversa da `static` e uno `z-index`, oppure con `transform`, `filter`, `opacity` minore di 1 e poche altre proprietà, ne crea uno nuovo per tutti i suoi discendenti. Se l'header ha per esempio `position: relative; z-index: 1` e la hero ha `z-index: 2`, il 9999 del menu vale solo "dentro l'header", e l'header intero sta sotto la hero.

La soluzione è alzare l'header, non il menu:

```css
.header {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 10;
}

.hero {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 1;
}

.menu {
  position: absolute;
  top: 100%;
  z-index: 1;
}
```

Per trovare chi crea il contesto, risali gli antenati del menu negli strumenti per sviluppatori e cerca `z-index`, `transform`, `filter` o `opacity` nei Computed. Uno dei casi più comuni è un'animazione con `transform` sulla hero, che le dà un proprio contesto.

Usare numeri piccoli e pochi livelli (per esempio 1 per i contenuti, 10 per l'header, 100 per le modali) evita la corsa al 9999.

A 1920x1080 servono dimensioni da proiezione, circa il doppio di quelle da pagina web: titoli intorno ai 64px, testo intorno ai 34px e mai sotto i 24-28px per note e didascalie.

```css
.slide {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  width: 1920px;
  height: 1080px;
  padding: 90px 100px;
  font-size: 34px;
  line-height: 1.35;
}
.slide h2 {
  font-size: 64px;
  line-height: 1.1;
  margin: 0 0 48px;
}
.slide .sottotitolo {
  font-size: 44px;
}
.slide .nota {
  font-size: 28px;
}
```

Con 34px di testo in una slide ci stanno 4-6 righe: è un limite utile, non un difetto. Anche i margini vanno scalati: 100px ai lati sembrano tanti nel codice, ma su uno schermo proiettato corrispondono al respiro normale di una slide.

Per vedere le slide intere su qualsiasi finestra, lascia il canvas a 1920x1080 e scalalo invece di cambiare le dimensioni:

```css
.deck {
  width: 1920px;
  height: 1080px;
  transform-origin: top left;
}
```

```js
function adatta() {
  const s = Math.min(innerWidth / 1920, innerHeight / 1080);
  document.querySelector(".deck").style.transform = `scale(${s})`;
}
addEventListener("resize", adatta);
adatta();
```

Se prendi le misure da un template PowerPoint, ricorda che lì sono in punti: si convertono con px = pt × 1,333, quindi un titolo a 48pt diventa 64px.

sta funzione calcola la media dei voti per ogni studente, va bene? se vuoi dimmi anche cose di stile

```python
def media_voti(studenti):
    risultati = {}
    for s in studenti:
        risultati[s["nome"]] = round(sum(s["voti"]) / len(s["voti"]), 2)
    return risultati
```
